## snp2.5
snp2.5:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
snp2.5:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Gayā on the cut-stone ledge in the haunt of Spiky the native spirit. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying in Gayā at Ṭaṁkitamañca, the haunt of Suciloma [NeedleHair] the yakkha.
snp2.5:1.3 | S: Now at that time the native spirits Shaggy and Spiky were passing by not far from the Buddha. | T: And on that occasion Khara [Rough] the yakkha and Suciloma the yakkha passed by not far from the Blessed One.
snp2.5:1.4 | S: So Shaggy said to Spiky, | T: Khara the yakkha said to Suciloma the yakkha,
snp2.5:1.5 | S: “That’s an ascetic.” | T: “That’s a contemplative.”
snp2.5:1.6 | S: “That’s no ascetic, he’s a faker! | T: “That’s not a contemplative. That’s a fake contemplative.
snp2.5:1.7 | S: I’ll soon find out whether he’s an ascetic or a faker.” | T: I’ll find out whether that’s a contemplative or a fake contemplative.”
snp2.5:2.1 | S: Then Spiky went up to the Buddha and leaned up against his body, | T: So Suciloma the yakkha approached the Blessed One and on arrival leaned his body up against the Blessed One.
snp2.5:2.2 | S: but the Buddha pulled away. | T: The Blessed One leaned his body away.
snp2.5:2.3 | S: Then Spiky said to the Buddha, | T: So Suciloma the yakkha said to the Blessed One,
snp2.5:2.4 | S: “Are you afraid, ascetic?” | T: “Are you afraid of me, contemplative?”
snp2.5:2.5 | S: “No, respectable sir, I’m not afraid. | T: “No, I’m not afraid of you, friend,
snp2.5:2.6 | S: But your touch is nasty.” | T: just that your touch is evil.”
snp2.5:3.1 | S: “I will ask you a question, ascetic. | T: “I will ask you a question, contemplative.
snp2.5:3.2 | S: If you don’t answer me, I’ll drive you insane, or explode your heart, or grab you by the feet and throw you to the far shore of the Ganges!” | T: If you can’t answer me, I will hurl out your mind or rip open your heart or, grabbing you by the feet, hurl you across the Ganges.”
snp2.5:4.1 | S: “I don’t see anyone in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans who could do that to me. | T: “My friend, I see no one in the cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, who could hurl out my mind or rip open my heart or, grabbing me by the feet, hurl me across the Ganges.
snp2.5:4.2 | S: But anyway, ask what you wish.” | T: But nevertheless, ask me what you wish.”
snp2.5:4.3 | S: Then Spiky addressed the Buddha in verse: | T: So Suciloma the yakkha addressed the Blessed One in verse:
snp2.5:5.1 | S: “Where do greed and hate come from? | T: Passion & aversion come from what cause?
snp2.5:5.2 | S: From where spring discontent, desire, and terror? | T: Displeasure, delight, horripilation are born from what?
snp2.5:5.3 | S: Where do the mind’s thoughts originate, | T: Arising from what do thoughts fling the mind around,
snp2.5:5.4 | S: like a crow let loose by boys.” | T: as boys, a (captive) crow?
snp2.5:6.1 | S: “Greed and hate come from here; | T: The Buddha: Passion & aversion come from this as a cause;
snp2.5:6.2 | S: from here spring discontent, desire, and terror; | T: displeasure, delight, horripilation are born from this;
snp2.5:6.3 | S: here’s where the mind’s thoughts originate, | T: arising from this thoughts fling the mind around,
snp2.5:6.4 | S: like a crow let loose by boys. | T: as boys, a (captive) crow:
snp2.5:7.1 | S: Born of affection, originating in oneself, | T: They’re born from affection arisen from the mind, from oneself,
snp2.5:7.2 | S: like prop roots sprouting from a banyan; | T: like the trunk-born (shoots) of a banyan tree,
snp2.5:7.3 | S: the many kinds of attachment to sensual pleasures | T: thick, attached to sensuality,
snp2.5:7.4 | S: are like camel’s foot creeper <j>creeping through the woods. | T: like a māluva vine spread in a forest.
snp2.5:8.1 | S: Those who understand where they come from | T: Those who discern where it’s born
snp2.5:8.2 | S: dispel them—listen up, spirit! | T: drive it out—listen, yakkha!
snp2.5:8.3 | S: They cross this flood so hard to cross, | T: They cross over this flood, so hard to cross,
snp2.5:8.4 | S: not crossed before, so as to not be reborn.” | T: never crossed before, for the sake of no further becoming.
snp2.5:8.5 | P: Sūcilomasuttaṁ pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp4.11
snp4.11:1.1 | S: “Where do quarrels and disputes come from? | T: “From where have there arisen quarrels, disputes,
snp4.11:1.2 | S: And lamentation and sorrow, and stinginess? | T: lamentation, sorrows, along with stinginess,
snp4.11:1.3 | S: What of conceit and arrogance, and slander too— | T: conceit & pride, along with divisiveness?
snp4.11:1.4 | S: tell me please, where do they come from?” | T: From where have they arisen? Please tell me.”
snp4.11:2.1 | S: “Quarrels and disputes come from what we hold dear, | T: “From what is dear there have arisen quarrels, disputes,
snp4.11:2.2 | S: as do lamentation and sorrow, stinginess, | T: lamentation, sorrows, along with stinginess,
snp4.11:2.3 | S: conceit and arrogance. | T: conceit & pride, along with divisiveness.
snp4.11:2.4 | S: Quarrels and disputes are linked to stinginess, | T: Tied up with stinginess are quarrels & disputes.
snp4.11:2.5 | S: and when disputes have arisen there is slander.” | T: In the arising of disputes is divisiveness.”
snp4.11:3.1 | S: “So where do things held dear <j>in the world spring from? | T: “Where is the cause of things dear in the world,
snp4.11:3.2 | S: And the lusts that are loose in the world? | T: along with the greeds that go about in the world?
snp4.11:3.3 | S: Where spring the hopes and aims | T: And where is the cause of the hopes & aims
snp4.11:3.4 | S: a man has for lives to come?” | T: for the sake of a person’s next life?”
snp4.11:4.1 | S: “What we hold dear in the world spring from desire, | T: “Desires are the cause of things dear in the world,
snp4.11:4.2 | S: as do the lusts that are loose in the world. | T: along with the greeds that go about in the world.
snp4.11:4.3 | S: From there spring the hopes and aims | T: And here too is the cause of the hopes & aims
snp4.11:4.4 | S: a man has for lives to come.” | T: for the sake of a person’s next life.”
snp4.11:5.1 | S: “So where does desire in the world spring from? | T: “Now where is the cause of desire in the world?
snp4.11:5.2 | S: And evaluations, too, where do they come from? | T: And from where have there arisen decisions,
snp4.11:5.3 | S: And anger, lies, and indecision, | T: anger, lies, & perplexity,
snp4.11:5.4 | S: and other things spoken of by the Ascetic?” | T: and all the qualities described by the Contemplative?”
snp4.11:6.1 | S: “What they call pleasure and pain in the world— | T: “What they call ‘appealing’ & ‘unappealing’ in the world:
snp4.11:6.2 | S: based on that, desire comes about. | T: In dependence on that, desire arises.
snp4.11:6.3 | S: Seeing the manifestation and vanishing of forms, | T: Having seen becoming & not- with regard to forms,
snp4.11:6.4 | S: a personage makes evaluations in the world. | T: a person gives rise to decisions in the world;
snp4.11:7.1 | S: Anger, lies, and indecision— | T: anger, lies, & perplexity:
snp4.11:7.2 | S: these things are, too, when that pair is present. | T: these qualities, too, when there exists that very pair.
snp4.11:7.3 | S: One who is indecisive should train <j>in the path of knowledge; | T: A person perplexed should train for the path of knowledge,
snp4.11:7.4 | S: it is from knowledge <j>that the Ascetic speaks of these things.” | T: for it’s in having known that the Contemplative has spoken of qualities/dhammas.”
snp4.11:8.1 | S: “Where do pleasure and pain spring from? | T: “Where is the cause of appealing & un-?
snp4.11:8.2 | S: When what is absent do these things not occur? | T: When what isn’t do they not exist?
snp4.11:8.3 | S: And also, on the topic of <j>manifesting and vanishing— | T: And whatever is meant by becoming & not- :
snp4.11:8.4 | S: tell me where they spring from.” | T: Tell me, Where is their cause?”
snp4.11:9.1 | S: “Pleasure and pain spring from contact; | T: “Contact is the cause of appealing & un-.
snp4.11:9.2 | S: when contact is absent they do not occur. | T: When contact isn’t, they do not exist,
snp4.11:9.3 | S: And on the topic of <j>manifesting and vanishing— | T: along with what’s meant by becoming & not- :
snp4.11:9.4 | S: I tell you they spring from there.” | T: I tell you, from here is their cause.”
snp4.11:10.1 | S: “So where does contact in the world spring from? | T: “Now where is the cause of contact in the world,
snp4.11:10.2 | S: And possessions, too, where do they come from? | T: and from where have graspings, possessions, arisen?
snp4.11:10.3 | S: When what is absent is there no possessiveness? | T: When what isn’t does there not exist mine-ness?
snp4.11:10.4 | S: When what vanishes do contacts not strike?” | T: When what has disappeared do contacts not touch?”
snp4.11:11.1 | S: “Name and form cause contact; | T: “Conditioned by name-&-form is contact.
snp4.11:11.2 | S: possessions spring from wishing; | T: In longing do graspings, possessions have their cause.
snp4.11:11.3 | S: when wishing is absent there is no possessiveness; | T: When longing isn’t, mine-ness doesn’t exist.
snp4.11:11.4 | S: when form vanishes, contacts don’t strike.” | T: When forms have disappeared contacts don’t touch.”
snp4.11:12.1 | S: “Form vanishes for one proceeding how? | T: “For one how-arriving does form disappear?
snp4.11:12.2 | S: And how do happiness and suffering vanish? | T: How do pleasure & pain disappear?
snp4.11:12.3 | S: Tell me how they vanish; | T: Tell me this.
snp4.11:12.4 | S: I think we ought to know these things.” | T: My heart is set on knowing how they disappear.”
snp4.11:13.1 | S: “Without normal perception <j>or distorted perception; | T: “One not percipient of perceptions not percipient of aberrant perceptions,
snp4.11:13.2 | S: not lacking perception, <j>nor perceiving what has vanished. | T: not unpercipient, nor percipient of what’s disappeared:
snp4.11:13.3 | S: Form vanishes for one proceeding thus; | T: For one thus-arriving, form disappears—
snp4.11:13.4 | S: for judgments due to proliferation <j>spring from perception.” | T: for objectification-classifications have their cause in perception.”
snp4.11:14.1 | S: “Whatever I asked you have explained to me. | T: “What we have asked, you’ve expounded to us.
snp4.11:14.2 | S: I ask you once more, please tell me this: | T: We ask one thing more. Please tell it.
snp4.11:14.3 | S: Do some astute folk here say that this <j>is the highest extent | T: Do some of the wise say that just this much is the utmost,
snp4.11:14.4 | S: of purity of the spirit? | T: that purity of spirit is here?
snp4.11:14.5 | S: Or do they say it is something else?” | T: Or do they say that it’s other than this?”
snp4.11:15.1 | S: “Some astute folk do say that this <j>is the highest extent | T: “Some of the wise say that just this much is the utmost,
snp4.11:15.2 | S: of purity of the spirit. | T: that purity of spirit is here.
snp4.11:15.3 | S: But some of them, claiming to be experts, | T: But some of them, who say they are skilled,
snp4.11:15.4 | S: speak of an occasion when there is no residue. | T: say it’s the moment with no clinging remaining.
snp4.11:16.1 | S: Knowing that these states are dependent, | T: But knowing, ‘Having known, they still are dependent,’
snp4.11:16.2 | S: and knowing what they depend on, <j>the inquiring sage, | T: the sage ponders dependencies.
snp4.11:16.3 | S: having understood, is freed, and enters no dispute. | T: On knowing them, released, he doesn’t get into disputes,
snp4.11:16.4 | S: The attentive do not proceed to life after life.” | T: doesn’t meet with becoming & not- : He’s enlightened.”
snp4.11:16.5 | P: Kalahavivādasuttaṁ ekādasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp4.2
snp4.2:1.1 | S: Trapped in a cave, thickly overspread, | T: Staying attached to the cave, covered heavily over,
snp4.2:1.2 | S: sunk in delusion they stay. | T: a person sunk in confusion
snp4.2:1.3 | S: That sort is far from seclusion, | T: is far from seclusion—
snp4.2:1.4 | S: for sensual pleasures in the world <j>are not easy to give up. | T: for sensual pleasures sensual desires in the world are not lightly let go.
snp4.2:2.1 | S: The chains of desire, the bonds of life’s pleasures | T: Those chained by desire, bound by becoming’s allure,
snp4.2:2.2 | S: are hard to escape, for one cannot free another. | T: aren’t easily released for there’s no liberation by others.
snp4.2:2.3 | S: Looking to the past or the future, | T: Intent, in front or behind,
snp4.2:2.4 | S: they pray for these pleasures or former ones. | T: on hunger for sensual pleasures here or before—
snp4.2:3.1 | S: Greedy, fixated, infatuated by sensual pleasures, | T: greedy for sensual pleasures, busy, deluded,
snp4.2:3.2 | S: they are incorrigible, habitually immoral. | T: ungenerous, entrenched in the discordant way,
snp4.2:3.3 | S: When led to suffering they lament, | T: they—impelled into pain—lament:
snp4.2:3.4 | S: “What will become of us <j>when we pass away from here?” | T: “What will we be when we pass on from here?”
snp4.2:4.1 | S: That’s why a personage should train in this life: | T: So a person should train right here-&-now.
snp4.2:4.2 | S: should you know that anything in the world is wrong, | T: Whatever you know as discordant in the world,
snp4.2:4.3 | S: don’t act wrongly on account of that; | T: don’t, for its sake, act discordantly,
snp4.2:4.4 | S: for the attentive say this life is short. | T: for that life, the enlightened say, is short.
snp4.2:5.1 | S: I see the world’s population floundering, | T: I see them, in the world, floundering around,
snp4.2:5.2 | S: given to craving for future lives. | T: people immersed in craving for states of becoming.
snp4.2:5.3 | S: Base men wail in the jaws of death, | T: Base people moan in the mouth of death,
snp4.2:5.4 | S: not rid of craving for life after life. | T: their craving, for states of becoming & not-, unallayed.
snp4.2:6.1 | S: See them flounder over belongings, | T: See them, floundering in their sense of mine,
snp4.2:6.2 | S: like fish in puddles of a dried-up stream. | T: like fish in the puddles of a dried-up stream—
snp4.2:6.3 | S: Seeing this, live unselfishly, | T: and, seeing this, live with no mine,
snp4.2:6.4 | S: forming no attachment to future lives. | T: not forming attachment for states of becoming.
snp4.2:7.1 | S: Rid of desire for both ends, | T: Subdue desire for both sides,
snp4.2:7.2 | S: having completely understood contact, free of greed, | T: comprehending sensory contact, with no greed.
snp4.2:7.3 | S: doing nothing for which they’d blame themselves, | T: Doing nothing for which he himself would rebuke himself,
snp4.2:7.4 | S: the attentive don’t cling to the seen and the heard. | T: the enlightened person doesn’t adhere to what’s seen, to what’s heard.
snp4.2:8.1 | S: Having completely understood perception <j>and having crossed the flood, | T: Comprehending perception, he’d cross over the flood—
snp4.2:8.2 | S: the sage, not clinging to possessions, | T: the sage not stuck on possessions.
snp4.2:8.3 | S: with dart plucked out, living diligently, | T: Then, with arrow removed, living heedfully,
snp4.2:8.4 | S: does not hope for this world or the next. | T: he longs for neither— this world, the next.
snp4.2:8.5 | P: Guhaṭṭhakasuttaṁ dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

