## thag2.11
thag2.11:1.1 | S: It’s from eagerness to learn that learning grows; | T: Listening well increases learning.
thag2.11:1.2 | S: when you’re learned, wisdom grows; | T: What is learned increases discernment.
thag2.11:1.3 | S: by wisdom, you know the goal; | T: Through discernment one knows the goal.
thag2.11:1.4 | S: knowing the goal brings happiness. | T: When known, the goal brings bliss.
thag2.11:2.1 | S: You should frequent remote lodgings | T: Stay in isolated lodgings;
thag2.11:2.2 | S: and practice to be released from fetters. | T: live for freedom from fetters.
thag2.11:2.3 | S: If you don’t find enjoyment there, | T: If you don’t attain delight there,
thag2.11:2.4 | S: live in the Saṅgha, self-protected and mindful. | T: then live in the Saṅgha, your mind protected, mindful.
thag2.11:2.5 | P: … Mahācundo thero …. | T: (none)

## thag2.16
thag2.16:1.1 | S: There’s a big black woman who looks like a crow. | T: This swarthy woman [preparing a corpse for cremation] —crow-like, enormous—
thag2.16:1.2 | S: She broke off thigh-bones, first one then another; | T: breaking a thigh & then the other thigh,
thag2.16:1.3 | S: she broke off arm-bones, first one then another; | T: breaking an arm & then the other arm,
thag2.16:1.4 | S: she broke off a skull like a curd-bowl, and then | T: cracking open the head, like a pot of curds,
thag2.16:1.5 | S: arranged them and sat nearby. | T: she sits with them heaped up beside her.
thag2.16:2.1 | S: When an ignorant person builds up attachments, | T: Whoever, unknowing, makes acquisitions
thag2.16:2.2 | S: that dullard returns to suffering again and again. | T: —the fool— returns over & over to suffering & stress.
thag2.16:2.3 | S: So let one who understands <j>not build up attachments: | T: So, discerning, don’t make acquisitions.
thag2.16:2.4 | S: may I never again lie with a broken skull! | T: May I never lie with my head cracked open again.
thag2.16:2.5 | P: … Mahākāḷo thero …. | T: (none)

