## thag4.10
thag4.10:1.1 | S: “Dhamma surely protects <j>one who practices Dhamma. | T: The Dhamma protects those who live by the Dhamma.
thag4.10:1.2 | S: The teaching brings happiness when practiced well. | T: The Dhamma well-practiced brings bliss.
thag4.10:1.3 | S: This is the benefit of practicing Dhamma: | T: This—the reward when the Dhamma’s well-practiced:
thag4.10:1.4 | S: one doesn’t go to a bad destination. | T: One who lives by the Dhamma doesn’t go to a bad destination.
thag4.10:2.1 | S: It’s not the case that Dhamma <j>and what is not Dhamma | T: For Dhamma and non-
thag4.10:2.2 | S: lead to the same results. | T: don’t bear equal results.
thag4.10:2.3 | S: What is not Dhamma leads to hell, | T: Non-Dhamma leads you to hell;
thag4.10:2.4 | S: while Dhamma takes you to a good place. | T: Dhamma, to a good destination.
thag4.10:3.1 | S: So you should rouse up enthusiasm for the teachings; | T: So you should engender desire for acts of Dhamma,
thag4.10:3.2 | S: such rejoicing is owing to the Holy One, <j>the unaffected. | T: rejoicing in the One Well-Gone, the one who is Such.
thag4.10:3.3 | S: The disciples of the excellent Holy One <j>are firm in the teaching; | T: Standing firm in the Dhamma, of the foremost One Well-Gone, his disciples
thag4.10:3.4 | S: those attentive ones are led on, <j>headed to the very best of refuges.” | T: are guided —enlightened— to the foremost refuge supreme.
thag4.10:4.1 | S: “The boil has been burst from its root, | T: Burst is the root of the boil;
thag4.10:4.2 | S: the net of craving is eradicated. | T: the net of craving uprooted.
thag4.10:4.3 | S: He has ended transmigration, he has nothing, | T: He, having ended his wandering-on, has no stain—
thag4.10:4.4 | S: he’s like the full moon on a bright night.” | T: like the moon on a clear full-moon night.
thag4.10:4.5 | P: … Dhammiko thero …. | T: (none)

